<%# locals: (key:, label:, set:, options: [], locked_reason: nil, clearable: true, filterable: false, footer: nil, open: false, notice: nil, handle: 'filter-pill', clear_url: nil, clear_owns: nil, align: :start) %>

<%# ONE choice of a filter line (S7, finding F7): a pill that carries its own
    name and its value, opening a menu of the values it can take.

    SHARED since P2 of the billings redesign: the time list renders it five
    times (period, project, subject, billing, display chooser) and the billings
    list renders its own line from it. Written out per filter, the surfaces had
    every chance to disagree about what "set" looks like, which is how the
    period row and the status row came to disagree about the muted tone before
    S2. What a clearing click CHANGES is the caller's knowledge, not this
    partial's: the caller passes clear_url (the no-JS fallback address) and
    clear_owns (the owns pairs for the live rewrite) instead of this file
    reaching into one page's removal map.

    Three states, and each one says something different (F7):
      set     -- filled, ink border, bold. Carries the value and a ✕.
      open    -- outline only. Selectable, nothing selected.
      locked  -- dampened, not a control at all, and it says why.
    The tones live in CHOICE_STATES, one map for the four surfaces.

    ⚠️ A <details>, not a JavaScript menu. The period used to be nine links and
    the project a native select, so the filter worked with no JavaScript at all;
    a menu that only Stimulus can open would have taken that away. <details>
    opens on its own, is keyboard-operable on its own, and details-autoclose
    (the org switcher's controller) only ADDS closing on an outside click and on
    Escape. The options stay plain links, which is also what lets query-toggle
    rewrite them against the live address.

    ⚠️ The ✕ sits INSIDE the summary, so clicking it would toggle the menu open
    on its way to following the link. details-autoclose#stop is exactly the
    affordance for that (the sidebar's gear does the same). %>

<% if locked_reason.present? %>
  <%# Dampened instead of dropped (E2). The rule is unchanged and still lives in
      TimeSessionsHelper#time_billing_choice_locked?; only its answer moved from
      "gone" to "not now, and here is why". Dropping the control made the row
      jump and explained nothing, which is the same complaint F7 raises about
      the subject select appearing on project selection.
      A <span> with a title, not a disabled <button>: there is nothing to press.
      The reason travels as text for screen readers, because a title is only
      reachable with a mouse. %>
  <span class="<%= time_filter_choice_pill_classes(:empty) %>"
        title="<%= locked_reason %>"
        data-<%= handle %>="<%= key %>"
        data-<%= handle %>-state="locked">
    <span><%= label %></span>
    <i class="bi bi-slash-circle text-[10px]" aria-hidden="true"></i>
    <span class="sr-only"><%= locked_reason %></span>
  </span>
<% else %>
  <%# `open` exists for one case: the custom period. Choosing it is a page visit,
      and its two date fields live in this menu -- so on the answer the reader
      would face a closed menu and no way to type the dates they just asked for.
      Rendered open, the fields are simply there, with or without JavaScript. %>
  <%# min-w-0 max-w-full on the pill, its summary and its label: a chosen
      value with a long name ("Internationales Kundenprojekt mit ...") made the
      pill wider than the whole filter column on a phone, because the label's
      own cap (15rem) does not count the padding and the ✕. Now the label gives
      way first and ends in an ellipsis (review of #932). %>
  <details class="relative inline-block min-w-0 max-w-full"
           <%= 'open' if open %>
           data-controller="details-autoclose"
           data-<%= handle %>="<%= key %>"
           data-<%= handle %>-state="<%= set ? 'set' : 'open' %>">
    <summary class="<%= time_filter_choice_pill_classes(set ? :active : :open) %> max-w-full list-none cursor-pointer select-none">
      <%# Capped and clipped: a ticket or meeting title goes in here, and a long
          one would push the rest of the line off the screen. %>
      <span class="min-w-0 max-w-[15rem] truncate"><%= label %></span>

      <% if set && clearable && clear_url.present? %>
        <%= link_to clear_url,
                    data: time_filter_owns(clear_owns)
                            .merge(action: 'click->query-toggle#rewrite click->details-autoclose#stop'),
                    class: 'inline-flex flex-shrink-0 items-center justify-center -mr-1 rounded-full hover:bg-cream-300' do %>
          <i class="bi bi-x text-[13px]" aria-hidden="true"></i>
          <%# The glyph says "remove" to a mouse and nothing to a screen reader. %>
          <span class="sr-only"><%= t('views.organisations.time_sessions.empty_reason.remove') %></span>
        <% end %>
      <% else %>
        <i class="bi bi-chevron-down flex-shrink-0 text-[10px]" aria-hidden="true"></i>
      <% end %>
    </summary>

    <%# ⚠️ The height cap is relative to the VIEWPORT, not a fixed 20rem. Nine
        period presets plus the custom date panel are taller than that, so the
        one menu with a KNOWN, small set was the one that scrolled -- and the
        custom fields, which are the whole point of opening it, sat below the
        fold. A long menu (subject, billing) still needs a cap; a short one must
        never get one it can hit.

        ⚠️ Deliberately NOT the brutalist frame the rest of the page wears. A 2px
        ink border with a hard 4px shadow reads as an object on the page, and this
        is a layer over it: it is the heaviest thing on screen the moment it opens,
        for something the reader means to dismiss in a second. Soft border, soft
        shadow, room to breathe. Design direction of the user, 02.09.2026. %>
    <%# ⚠️ `align` is about the PAGE, not about taste. The menu is at least 16rem
        wide and hangs off one edge of its own pill, so a pill that has been
        pushed to the end of its row (ms-auto, or last in a justify-between row)
        opens a menu that runs off the right side of the page. Which edge it
        hangs from is therefore the caller's knowledge - it is the only one that
        knows where its pill sits - and `align: :end` is the answer for the two
        that sit at the end: the billing chooser in the time list's filter row
        and the display chooser in the tool row.
        Logical properties (start/end), not left/right: the layout carries rtl:
        variants, and a menu that flips with the text direction is one less
        thing to remember. %>
    <%# ⚠️ The width is capped to the screen as well (min() with the viewport
        less 1rem), and details-autoclose moves the menu sideways into view
        whenever its size or the screen's changes (the `menu` target): on a
        phone a pill can sit anywhere in a wrapped row, and a menu hanging from
        it reached past the edge (reviews of #932). %>
    <div class="absolute <%= align.to_sym == :end ? 'end-0' : 'start-0' %> top-full mt-2 z-dropdown min-w-[min(16rem,calc(100vw_-_1rem))] max-w-[min(24rem,calc(100vw_-_1rem))] overflow-y-auto bg-cream-50 border border-cream-300 shadow-[0_10px_30px_-8px_rgba(20,17,12,0.28)] rounded-2xl py-2 <%= filterable ? 'max-h-[60vh]' : 'max-h-[75vh]' %>"
         <%# Mounted on every menu, literally, for two reasons: the wiring census
             (spec/architecture/stimulus_controller_wiring_spec.rb) reads mounts
             as literals and cannot see one built from a Ruby condition, and the
             controller is inert without a query field anyway -- its only entry
             point is that input's own action. %>
         data-controller="filter-menu-search"
         data-details-autoclose-target="menu">
      <%= render 'shared/filter_pill_options', options: options, filterable: filterable, handle: handle %>

      <% if notice.present? %>
        <%# A menu showing a WINDOW has to say so, or its silence is a claim that
            these are all the options there are. Below the options, not above
            them: it explains what the reader has just failed to find, and the
            first thing a reader wants is the list itself. %>
        <p class="px-3 py-1.5 mt-1 border-t border-cream-300 text-xs text-cream-600"><%= notice %></p>
      <% end %>

      <%= footer %>

    </div>
  </details>
<% end %>
